{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a174d76cfba6c631c","tool_use_id":"toolu_01VLWnW1MomfYKAwwuJFLFR1","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":91843,"tool_uses":26,"duration_ms":226224},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"eb1377b8-60d1-495a-886d-1e5da1076d10","session_id":"56dac4e5-2ce5-4fbd-b248-0bfac99e4894"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017Wo24B3PpCJgtPXeufibsN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01M6zamVtX7c2tuAcR9Hta9P","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"nlp_variance_gtheory","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260520T190053","overall_score":"borderline reject","report":"\n# Reviewer R1: Methods and Experiments\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to decompose variance in LLM evaluation across infrastructure (numerical precision), methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering), and measurement (item selection) facets. Across 4 models, 5 benchmarks, and 172,800+ records per full-crossing experiment, the authors report that variance attribution is benchmark-conditional and model-conditional: on MMLU item_id=24% is the largest attributable source after confound correction, while on GSM8K and MATH seed x item and temperature x item interactions dominate. A float32 engine confound in vLLM is identified and corrected. D-study projections show that optimal measurement allocations diverge qualitatively across benchmarks, undermining any universal reproducibility recipe. A fine-tuning extension (RoBERTa-large, 1,728 runs) reveals an inverted variance hierarchy where learning rate (17.8%) and lr x seed interaction (10%) dominate.\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n1. **Scale and systematic design.** The fully-crossed factorial on MMLU (4 models x 3 precisions x 6 prompts x 6 seeds x 3 temperatures x 4 orderings x 200 items = 172,800 records) with consistent confound control (V0 enforce_eager across all precision modes) is a genuinely impressive experimental effort. The extension across 5 benchmarks and 6 models (4x8B + 2x14B) provides breadth rarely seen in evaluation methodology papers.\n\n2. **Float32 engine confound discovery is a standalone contribution.** The identification that vLLM routes float32 to V1 backend versus V0 for BF16/FP16, inflating item variance from 24% to 58% (pre-fix MVP value), is a valuable methodological finding. The before/after comparison (Table 1 vs. MVP) is convincing and has immediate practical implications for any study using vLLM with mixed precision conditions. This finding alone justifies a methods paper.\n\n3. **Honest reframing around negative results.** The title and framing acknowledge that the original hypothesis (item dominance as universal) did not survive cross-benchmark replication. Reporting that item dominance fails on 0/4 benchmarks outside MMLU (Table 4 in cross-benchmark replication) and that variance structure is benchmark-conditional is scientifically responsible.\n\n4. **Benchmark-conditional variance gradient is well-supported.** The systematic pattern across 5 benchmarks -- item_id declining (MMLU 24% -> GSM8K 12% -> MATH 10%), residual increasing (28% -> 35% -> 40%), temperature x item increasing (5.5% -> 18% -> 15%) -- is a clean empirical finding with a plausible mechanistic account (CoT length amplifies sampling noise, r=0.65).\n\n5. **D-study allocation divergence is practically valuable.** The demonstration that optimal allocation strategies differ qualitatively across benchmarks (MMLU: 500 items x 2 prompts x 1 seed; GSM8K: 250 items x 4 prompts x 4 seeds for a fixed budget of 1000 conditions) is immediately actionable and fills a gap no prior work addresses.\n\n6. **Fine-tuning extension adds genuine novelty.** The first G-theory decomposition of fine-tuning variance with a fully-crossed design (6 seeds x 3 LR x 4 shuffles x 3 GPUs x 2 precisions) reveals learning rate dominance (17.8%) and the lr x seed interaction (10%). The inference-training variance correlation of r=0.15 is a useful negative finding showing that inference-based reproducibility budgets cannot guide training-time decisions.\n\n7. **Generation-based evaluation protocol.** Following Hua et al. (EMNLP 2025) to avoid log-likelihood scoring artifacts that inflate apparent prompt sensitivity reflects careful experimental design awareness.\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n**(W1) Over 54% of total variance is unexplained or aggregated in the MMLU analysis.** Table 1 shows residual=27.9% and \"all other\"=26.5%, meaning 54.4% of total variance is either unmodeled or lumped into an opaque category. The \"all other\" row is not defined -- it presumably aggregates minor two-way interactions and possibly some bookkeeping artifact, but the paper does not specify which components it contains or their individual magnitudes. For a paper whose entire contribution rests on variance attribution, having the majority of variance unattributed is a fundamental problem. The reader cannot assess whether item_id's 24% is genuinely the \"largest attributable source\" without knowing what is inside \"all other.\" If \"all other\" contains, say, a single three-way interaction at 15%, the variance hierarchy could look quite different.\n\n**(W2) Model is pooled rather than treated as a G-theory facet.** The paper pools 4 models and decomposes variance across the pooled data, but \"model\" is not included as a facet in the G-study. In G-theory, model should either be the object of measurement (differentiation facet) or a crossed facet. By pooling, model-level variance and model x item interaction are absorbed into item_id and residual respectively. The per-model analyses (Table 1 showing Mistral-7B with item=13.8% vs. Gemma-2 with item=30.2%) demonstrate that model x item interaction is substantial, yet this is never estimated as a separate variance component. The Mistral anomaly (residual=38.2%) likely reflects model x higher-order interactions being dumped into residual. This design choice fundamentally undermines the headline variance decomposition. A proper model-as-facet analysis would separate model x item interaction and almost certainly reduce item_id's attributed share further while revealing how much of the \"residual\" is actually structured model x condition interaction.\n\n**(W3) ANOVA-based estimation is used but the paper's methodological framing implies REML.** The code (`variance_decomposition.py`) implements classical ANOVA-based Henderson Method I estimation: it computes Type-I sums of squares, derives mean squares, and solves Expected Mean Square equations for variance components. Negative estimates are truncated to zero. This is not REML. REML uses restricted maximum likelihood via iterative optimization (e.g., Fisher scoring, EM) and handles boundary constraints differently (constrained optimization rather than truncation). The distilled research skill cites Mesting (2026) as using \"REML + D-study\" and positions this work as methodologically comparable. If the paper claims or implies REML while implementing ANOVA-based estimation, this is a misrepresentation that a psychometrics-aware reviewer would catch immediately. The two methods can yield different results, especially with unbalanced cells or when negative variance components arise (the truncation-to-zero approach inflates other components' relative shares).\n\n**(W4) Bootstrap CIs are structurally incomplete across experiments.** The code computes bootstrap CIs for all components on MMLU (exp-001), but the paper summary provides CIs only selectively. For the cross-benchmark experiments (exp-002), the 14B extension (exp-005), and the fine-tuning study (exp-006), no confidence intervals are reported. This is especially problematic for: (a) the separability claim, where \"direct infra x method < 0.5%\" needs a CI to be interpretable -- is the CI [0.0%, 3.0%] or [0.3%, 0.7%]?; (b) the cross-benchmark gradient, where overlapping CIs on item_id shares across benchmarks would weaken the \"qualitatively different\" claim; (c) the fine-tuning lr dominance claim (17.8%), where the CI width determines whether lr is statistically distinguishable from item_id (13.8%). Without CIs on all reported variance components, the paper is presenting point estimates as if they were precise measurements.\n\n**(W5) Experiment 3 (multi-hardware) is blocked, fatally weakening the separability claim.** The paper claims to test \"infrastructure vs. methodology separability\" but the only infrastructure facet actually crossed is numerical precision. Without a second GPU type, the paper cannot distinguish hardware-specific from hardware-universal variance. The separability claim must be scoped to \"precision vs. methodology\" rather than \"infrastructure vs. methodology.\" This is not merely a limitation -- it undermines the paper's positioning relative to its stated contribution of being the \"first fully-crossed G-theory study spanning infrastructure x methodology.\" With only precision as the infrastructure proxy, the study is methodologically closer to Mesting (2026) than advertised, differing primarily in adding precision as a facet rather than adding \"infrastructure\" broadly.\n\n### Moderate\n\n**(W6) Experiment 5 (14B) has no hardware crossing and only 2 models, making scale claims unreliable.** With n=2 (Qwen3-14B and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B), the 14B extension cannot separate model scale from architecture effects. DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B is a reasoning-distilled model with fundamentally different generation characteristics (item=10%, residual=42%), making it an inappropriate comparison partner for studying \"scale effects.\" The paper acknowledges this but still draws conclusions about scale invariance/variance that are confounded with architecture.\n\n**(W7) Temperature and precision levels differ across experiments without justification.** Exp-001 uses 3 precisions x 3 temperatures x 4 orderings on MMLU. Exp-002 uses 2 precisions x 2 temperatures on GSM8K/ARC/HellaSwag. This means the cross-benchmark comparison in Tables 3-4 is confounded with design differences: the reduced crossing on non-MMLU benchmarks means fewer levels of temperature and precision, which mechanically reduces their estimated variance shares. A fair cross-benchmark comparison requires identical factorial designs. The paper does not discuss how design asymmetry affects the comparability of variance component estimates.\n\n**(W8) All three-way and higher interactions are absorbed into the residual.** With 6 facets, there are 20 three-way interactions, 15 four-way, 6 five-way, and 1 six-way -- 42 higher-order terms total, all dumped into \"residual.\" The residual ranges from 20-40% across experiments. Some of this is likely structured: for instance, precision x temperature x item is plausible given that both precision x item (3.5%) and temperature x item (5.5%) are non-trivial on MMLU. If even one three-way interaction accounts for 5-8% of total variance, the relative ranking of two-way components would shift. The paper neither estimates selected three-way interactions nor discusses this confound in the residual.\n\n**(W9) D-study projections assume items are sampled from an infinite universe.** The G-theory D-study formula G = sigma_tau / (sigma_tau + sigma_delta/n) assumes random sampling from an infinite item universe. Benchmark items are curated, finite collections. For MATH (G=0.30, recommending 2200 items for G>=0.90 when only 5000 items exist in the full benchmark), the extrapolation is questionable. For GSM8K (recommending 1400 items), similar concerns apply. The paper acknowledges infeasibility but does not discuss the statistical invalidity of extrapolating finite-population variance components to hypothetical infinite-population D-study projections.\n\n**(W10) Fine-tuning experiment uses RoBERTa-large (355M, encoder-only) rather than a decoder-only model.** The architectural mismatch between the inference experiments (7-14B decoder-only autoregressive models) and the fine-tuning experiment (encoder-only classification model) weakens cross-paradigm generalization claims. Fine-tuning a decoder model (e.g., Llama-3.1-8B with LoRA on a generation task) would make the inference-vs-fine-tuning variance hierarchy comparison far more credible. As designed, the fine-tuning experiment measures a fundamentally different computational process (classification head fine-tuning vs. autoregressive generation), making the r=0.15 correlation between inference and training variance structures potentially an artifact of architectural mismatch rather than a deep insight about variance structure differences.\n\n### Minor\n\n**(W11) The Kruskal-Wallis test (H=47.3, p<0.001) is underspecified.** What is being compared? Per-item variance contributions across benchmarks? With k=5 benchmarks and point estimates derived from different experimental designs (different numbers of levels), the test's assumptions (independent samples from the same distribution family) may not hold. The degrees of freedom and sample sizes per group are not reported.\n\n**(W12) No multiple comparison correction.** Multiple bootstrap CIs and permutation tests are computed without family-wise error rate correction (Bonferroni, Holm, or FDR). With 11+ variance components per experiment and 7+ experiments, the number of implicit hypothesis tests is substantial.\n\n**(W13) The \"all other\" category in Table 1 (26.5%) is not decomposed.** This catch-all category is almost as large as residual (27.9%) and larger than item_id (24.0%). Presenting an aggregated \"all other\" without itemizing its constituents is unusual for a variance decomposition paper and prevents readers from understanding the full variance structure.\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. Why is \"model\" not included as a facet in the G-study? The per-model results (Table 1) show item_id ranges from 13.8% (Mistral) to 30.2% (Gemma-2) -- this 16.4pp range suggests substantial model x item interaction. If model were a facet, how much of the current item_id and residual estimates would be reclassified as model x item interaction?\n\n2. What exactly is in the \"all other\" category (26.5% in Table 1)? Please enumerate all components and their individual estimates. Is this a sum of small two-way interactions, or does it include terms that individually exceed 5%?\n\n3. The code (`variance_decomposition.py`) uses ANOVA-based Henderson Method I estimation with truncation of negative variance components to zero. Does the paper describe this correctly, or does it claim REML? If ANOVA-based, have you verified exact balance (every cell has the same number of observations) across all experiments?\n\n4. Why do the factorial designs differ between MMLU (3 precisions, 3 temperatures) and the other benchmarks (2 precisions, 2 temperatures)? How does this design asymmetry affect the comparability of cross-benchmark variance component estimates?\n\n5. Can you provide bootstrap CIs for the total direct infrastructure x methodology interaction on each benchmark? The 0.5% point estimate for separability requires uncertainty quantification.\n\n6. The D-study recommends 2200 items for MATH to reach G>=0.90, but MATH has only 5000 items total. At what point does the finite-population assumption break the D-study framework? Have you considered finite-population corrections?\n\n7. Have you tested any three-way interactions (e.g., precision x temperature x item, prompt x seed x item)? Given residual accounts for 28-40% across experiments, some of this may be structured and recoverable.\n\n8. For the fine-tuning experiment, why was RoBERTa-large chosen instead of fine-tuning one of the same decoder-only models used in the inference experiments (e.g., Llama-3.1-8B with LoRA)? How confident are you that the r=0.15 inference-training correlation reflects a genuine structural difference rather than an architectural mismatch artifact?\n\n9. The inference-training variance correlation (r=0.15) is computed between what exactly? Variance component shares? G coefficients? Per-item accuracy variance? The operationalization is unclear.\n\n10. Mistral-7B shows a qualitatively different variance profile (item=13.8%, residual=38.2%) compared to the other three 8B models. Is this anomaly investigated? Could it indicate that the G-theory facet structure is model-inappropriate for certain architectures?\n\n## 5. Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n1. **Model as a crossed facet.** Re-analyze the MMLU data with model as a 7th facet (or as the object of measurement). This requires no new data collection and would resolve the most fundamental methodological concern.\n\n2. **Three-way interaction estimation.** Fit at least the theoretically motivated three-way interactions (precision x temperature x item, prompt x seed x item, prompt x temperature x item) to determine how much of the residual is structured. This also requires no new data.\n\n3. **Dual-metric analysis.** Decision D002 describes radically different variance structures for binary correct (item_id=58.4%) vs. text exact match (prompt x item=37.5%). This analysis appears to have been planned but dropped. Including it would demonstrate metric sensitivity, which strengthens the \"no universal recipe\" argument.\n\n4. **Sensitivity analysis for the separability threshold.** Rather than a binary pass/fail at 0.5%, report the full bootstrap distribution of total direct infrastructure x methodology interaction and let readers judge.\n\n5. **ANOVA vs. REML comparison.** Run the same data through a proper REML implementation (e.g., lme4 in R, or statsmodels MixedLM in Python) and compare results. This validates or invalidates the ANOVA-based approach and is a single-afternoon analysis task.\n\n6. **Finite-population correction for D-study.** For benchmarks with small item pools (MATH: 5000, ARC: ~2590), apply finite-population corrections to the D-study projections and report adjusted G coefficients.\n\n7. **Decoder-only fine-tuning comparison.** Even a small-scale LoRA fine-tuning experiment with Llama-3.1-8B on one task would dramatically strengthen the inference-vs-fine-tuning comparison.\n\n## 6. Score\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe paper presents substantial experimental effort and several genuine contributions (engine confound discovery, benchmark-conditional variance gradient, D-study allocation divergence). However, the methodological execution has fundamental gaps: (1) over 54% of variance is unexplained or aggregated into opaque categories; (2) model is pooled rather than treated as a facet, confounding the core variance decomposition; (3) the estimation method may be misrepresented; (4) the blocked hardware experiment undermines the central \"infrastructure vs. methodology\" framing; (5) cross-benchmark comparisons use non-identical factorial designs. These are not cosmetic issues -- they affect the validity of all three central claims. The contributions that survive scrutiny (confound fix, benchmark gradient, fine-tuning decomposition) are individually interesting but do not cohere into a unified paper at the current level of methodological rigor. Most concerns are addressable without new experiments (model-as-facet reanalysis, three-way interactions, dual-metric analysis, proper CIs), but the revision scope is substantial.\n\n## 7. Confidence\n\n**Confidence: 4/5 (High)**\n\nI have expertise in experimental design, variance decomposition methods, and G-theory applications. I verified claims against the actual analysis code (`variance_decomposition.py`, `d_study.py`) and cross-referenced the experimental plan against reported results. My main uncertainty is whether the paper text (which I reviewed via summary rather than the full manuscript) accurately represents the estimation method -- the ANOVA-vs-REML concern is based on the code, which is definitive, but the paper's textual claims may differ from what I infer.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":732,"cache_read_input_tokens":90184,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":732,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VLWnW1MomfYKAwwuJFLFR1","session_id":"56dac4e5-2ce5-4fbd-b248-0bfac99e4894","uuid":"00d4e68f-4ec2-4d43-a192-93d0d56fb726"}
